[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is Airfoil, your flight, your story. It\'s a self-hosted personal flight tracker. Every flight you\'ve ever taken on an interactive world map with statistics about your route, airlines, aircraft, and travel patterns. Running on your server, in your database, owned'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by you. If you\'ve been logging flights in in Flighty or Flightradar24 and quietly wondering what happens to that history if the app dies or the subscription lapses, this is the answer and your old data comes along. How? Hold that'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thought. Here\'s the heart of it. The world map with your flight paths arcing between airports the way airline magazines used to draw them. Click a flight and the map focuses on that journey with its full details. And since'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the latest release, uploaded flight tracks can even be colored by altitude with dashed sections where the path was estimated. It handles light and dark mode, works on any screen size, and there\'s a live demo on the project site.'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It spins up a private throwaway instance for 10 minutes, so you can try the real thing before installing. The stack under it is modern SvelteKit and TypeScript with Postgres behind it. Snappy in a way electron air trackers'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'never were. The builder is Johan Oli from Copenhagen. 487 commits over 2 years on this one project with a small crew of community contributors around him. And here\'s what makes this hobby'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'project unusual. The readme carries SonarCloud quality gate, fossil license and security scans, an OpenSSF best practices badge, and an OpenSSF scorecard.'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That\'s supply chain hygiene most companies don\'t bother with on a flight logger someone builds after work. Adoption first. Nearly 700,000 Docker pulls for a personal flight tracker. 1,812'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'GitHub stars, 94 forks. The niche is specific. People who fly enough to want a record and self-host enough to want it at home. But within that niche, this is clearly become the default answer. The feature'

[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'set covers the whole ritual of flight logging. The map view for the romance, a flight list with full details for the record keeping, and statistics for the bragging rights. Total distance, time in the air, airlines, aircraft types,'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'visited countries. It\'s multi-user, so your partner or travel buddies get their own accounts, and you can share flights between them, and it plugs into your own auth provider if you run one. Flights can carry seat, class, and'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reason, and since the latest release, each passenger on a shared flight keeps their own seat and details. There\'s an airline and airport database built in, so entering a flight is usually just a flight number and date. The rest auto-fills. Install is the classic home'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lab two-step. The official compose file runs Postgres 16 and the Air Trail image. You set a database password and your origin URL in a .env file, and Docker compose up brings it to port'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '3000. First launch walks you through creating the owner account. You\'ll pick metric, imperial, or full aviation units. Nautical miles and meter conventions included, which tells you exactly who'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wrote this. Total footprint is tiny. This runs happily beside everything else on a Raspberry Pi or the smallest VPS you own. And the payoff on the hook, the importers. Air Trail reads your'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'history from eight sources. Flighty, TripIt, Radar 24, App in the Air, Jet Lag, By Air, Jet Lovers, and Open Flights.'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That matters more than it sounds because flight logging only works with your whole life in it. App in the Air already shut down once and stranded everyone\'s logs. That\'s the exact scenario self-hosting exists to end. Export from'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wherever you are, import once, and the years of history land on your own map. If you\'re starting from zero instead, adding flights manually is quick enough that an evening with your old boarding'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pass emails gets your history rebuilt. The switching conversation is the whole pitch in miniature. A decade of flights is a diary. Losing it hurts more than losing the app. Once it\'s in your post grads, no shutdown, no subscription'

[03:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'change, and no acquisition can touch it. Day-to-day, it lives on your phone. The responsive design means the map, your stats, and adding a flight from the gate all work in a mobile browser. Log the tail number while you\'re still boarding.'

[04:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And because it\'s your instance, checking your travel history isn\'t feeding anyone\'s analytics. The only person profiling your movements is you. Guests you fly with can get accounts, too, which turns the family vacation\'s map into a shared artifact instead of one'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'person\'s spreadsheet. Project health by the numbers. 1,512 stars, 94 forks, nearly 700,000 pulls, and nine open issues. Nine on a 2-year-old project with an install base this size. Combined'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with those quality badges, that\'s a maintainer who closes loops. The release history reads like a steady climb. Born August 2024, version 3.10 in May, 3.11 in June, and 3.12 in early August, the'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one that brought per flight detail panes, altitude colored tracks, and reworked passenger data. A release every month or two, each with real features for 2 years straight. The pace also means import formats keep pace'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the apps people are leaving. The newest importers arrive by community request. The honest context. Airtrail is GPL 3. Properly free, forks stay open. It\'s a solo maintainer project at heart,'

[05:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2 years young, so way that like any personal project dependency. Though the Open SSF scorecard, Sonar Gates, and security scans are exactly the counterweight you want to see. Remember what this data is? Your'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'complete travel movement history, which is precisely why you\'d self-host it rather than gift it to an ad-funded app. And the demo instances on the project site are throwaway by design, 10 minutes and deleted, so don\'t put anything real'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in one. One operational note, image uploads need a volume writable by user 1000, the classic Docker permissions footnote. The compose file documents it. If your'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'flight history lives in an app that could vanish, bring it home. Two containers, one import, and every trip you\'ve ever taken is on your own map for good. Repository and website linked in the description. If this earned a place'

